{"kind":"assert","text":"Ruling on request 50b962b9, identifiers (c+d). Two decisions. The\nfirst refines item 1b of ruling a94bfa4d.\n\n1. A retained first entry is a birth. Admit it as one.\n   A `SequenceRef` is `{scope, genesis}`, and `genesis` is the digest\n   of the first entry. So the bytes of a first entry prove the\n   sequence wherever they come from. The check is on content, and the\n   wire adds nothing to it.\n   a. When the fact of a retained use has `seq` 0, its retained bytes\n      go through the same birth admission that a fetched birth\n      passes, against `fact.at`. The result is a genuine\n      CheckedSequence. No read is added, and nothing is made up: the\n      admission fails if the bytes do not have that digest.\n   b. A later retained entry associates by `proofEntry` with a birth\n      that the same origin holds, fetched or retained. It is then\n      genuine too.\n   c. A later retained entry whose origin holds no birth of its\n      sequence stays a retained use. Items 1b to 1e of a94bfa4d apply\n      to that case only.\n   d. `rulesCertificateFamilyV17`, and the V14 and V15 families, take\n      genuine values only. Under items a and b they receive the three\n      births that main gives them, so `validConfirmed` holds and the\n      ledger selects as before. The witness is the existing one:\n      evidence-rules-real-fixture.ts keeps `selected=true`.\n   e. The head writes no `selected=false` that main does not write.\n      If a projection that requires a birth meets a value of case c\n      that main used, stop and name it. Do not write the outcome and\n      do not answer unavailable on your own choice.\n   This is what ruling 33c2c7c7 said of the archive roots: a\n   CheckedSequence comes from bytes already read.\n\n2. No local aggregate allowance. Confirmed as you recommend.\n   The retained rows keep the reads and the byte validation of main.\n   `readRetainedUses` takes no `maxBytes`, and the head adds no\n   rejection for a body that the store already holds. Each row passed\n   `entryBytes` when the scope admitted it. Cap A bounds one source\n   response on the wire and nothing else. A local ceiling is a\n   separate contract, and nobody has asked for one.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}